    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All tests pass. The implementation is complete and the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2/2 tests passing \u2014 `AcceptanceTest` is the only concrete test class; `AcceptanceBase` is abstract and `AuditLogCapture` is support).\n\n## Summary\n\nChanged owner deletion from a hard delete to a soft delete, with the create pipeline's duplicate/identity checks ignoring deleted owners. Each change sits in the unit that owns that responsibility:\n\n1. **`Owner` entity** (`model/Owner.java`) \u2014 added a `deleted` boolean column (`@Column(name = \"deleted\")`, defaulting to `Boolean.FALSE`), its getter/setter, and a small `isActive()` predicate (`!Boolean.TRUE.equals(deleted)`) reused by the checks.\n\n2. **`DeleteOwner`** function \u2014 now flags `owner.setDeleted(true)` and `save`s, instead of `ownerRepository.delete(owner)`. The DELETE pipeline is otherwise unchanged (still governed by a transaction, still responds 204 \u2014 a 2xx). The row is retained.\n\n3. **Duplicate/identity checks** \u2014 `EnsureUniqueIdentity` (the hard 409 block) and `FlagPossibleDuplicate` (the soft flag) now skip existing owners via `existing.isActive()`, so a soft-deleted match no longer blocks or flags a new create. The count steps (household size / namesake count) were deliberately left unchanged since they are metrics, not duplicate/identity checks.\n\n4. **OpenAPI schema** (`openapi.yml`) \u2014 added a read-only `deleted` boolean to the `Owner` response schema (not to `OwnerFields`, so it stays out of the create request). MapStruct auto-maps it from the entity, so `GET /api/owners/{id}` surfaces `$.deleted`.\n\n5. **DB schemas** \u2014 added `deleted BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE` to the `owners` table in all four dialects (hsqldb \u2014 used by tests \u2014 plus h2, postgres, mysql). The `DEFAULT FALSE` means the seed inserts (which omit the column) and newly built owners both persist `false`.\n\nBehavior now: a new owner reads `deleted: false`; `DELETE` flips it to `true` while `GET` still returns the record; and re-creating a normally-blocking duplicate succeeds when the only matching owner has been deleted.",
